 "spineImpact": {
  "_headline": "The spine classification survives almost intact. Exactly one object is affected.",
  "checked": "All 223 catalogued objects: 161 entity_scoped, 52 firm_global, 9 subtype_root, 1 supertype.",
  "consistency": "Of the 161 entity_scoped, all 148 present in this capture carry a ProjectEntityID typed 'Entity ID', and ZERO entity_scoped objects lack a ProjectEntityID column. The rule was applied consistently.",
  "theSingleException": {
   "object": "AssetHistory",
   "fields": 13,
   "catalogueSpine": "firm_global",
   "problem": "It DOES carry a ProjectEntityID column, but typed 'Entity' rather than 'Entity ID', so a type-matched scan does not see it.",
   "recommendation": "Likely mis-classified; should probably be entity_scoped. Verify before changing - AssetHistory is a snapshot of an Asset's financial state, and it is arguable whether it is scoped to the entity or to the Asset.",
   "evidence": "Observed"
  },
  "whyTheSubtypeRootsAreNotAffected": "Contract, Facility, Location, Parcel, Program, Project, Prototype, PotentialProject and ProjectEntity all carry ProjectEntityID typed 'Number' rather than 'Entity ID'. That is the 9 subtype_root objects plus the supertype - the catalogue already models them separately on a shared key, so this is consistent behaviour, not a defect."
 },
 "whyItMatters": [
  "Any FK graph built by matching type strings under-counts by ~87 edges (~9%). Impact analysis, orphan detection and in-degree are all computed from that graph.",
  "The Punch List family is the extreme case: all three children reference their parent through a Text-typed column, so the family renders as four disconnected tables. That is what surfaced the pattern.",
  "In-degree for the 30 under-counted targets is understated - Issue by 13, Firm by 12, ProjectEntity by 9. Issue and ProjectEntity are both central objects, so this is not confined to the periphery.",
  "For ASG Edge+: a rebuild that derives its FK model from declared column TYPES rather than from naming convention will silently lose these 87 relationships."
 ],
